#
# RoccoSidki: Rocco's nu(G), Sidki's chi(G) and related groups
#
# General declarations.
#
#! @Chapter Introduction
#!
#! The &RoccoSidki; package computes Rocco's group $\nu(G)$ and the groups
#! $\tau^q(G)$ of Dias and Rocco, Sidki's weak commutativity group $\chi(G)$,
#! the groups $\chi(n,H)$ and $\chi_n(H)$ with $n$ copies, and the hybrid group
#! $Q(H)$, together with their subgroups, and the groups $\chi(H,\sigma)$ of weak
#! commutativity defined by a bijection, with the reductions of Oliveira and
#! Sidki. All of them are quotients of the free
#! product of copies of $G$, so the input group may be given in any
#! representation (permutation, pc, pcp, matrix or finitely presented) and the
#! embeddings of the copies of $G$ are kept.
#!
#! This is a preliminary version. Planned for later versions: a polycyclic
#! presentation of $\nu^q(G)$ itself, and $\nu(G)$ for non-solvable groups.
#!
#! The package is a compilation of the &GAP; code that the author has been
#! writing since his doctoral studies under the supervision of Said N. Sidki
#! (Universidade de Brasília, 2007), and that he has used in his research
#! papers. The original functions are kept in the folder <F>original</F> of
#! the package, unchanged except for a first line giving their date; they are
#! not loaded.
#!
#! @Section Info class

#! @Description
#!   Info class of the package.
DeclareInfoClass( "InfoRoccoSidki" );

#! @Chapter How the groups are built
#!
#! @Section Free products with embeddings
#!
#! Every group of this package is a quotient $Q = (G_1 * \cdots * G_n)/N$ of a
#! free product of copies of the input group $G$, where $N$ is the normal
#! closure of the defining relations. The construction starts from
#! <C>FreeProduct</C> of &GAP;, which accepts groups in any representation, and
#! keeps the embeddings $G \to G_i$ given by <C>Embedding</C>. The images of the
#! generators of $G$ are carried through the quotient and through the
#! conversion of $Q$ into a pc group, and the embeddings $G \to Q$ are stored
#! with the result.
#!
#! This is what makes the subgroups of $Q$ cheap to compute. For instance, in
#! $\chi(G)$ the subgroups $L(G)$, $D(G)$, $W(G)$ and $R(G)$ are defined in terms of
#! the two copies $G$ and $G^\psi$ and of the isomorphism $\psi$ between them;
#! with the embeddings at hand they are direct computations inside a pc group.
#! Without them, one has to locate the copies of $G$ inside the computed group,
#! which is a search, or to carry every element of $G$ through a presentation
#! by hand.

#! @Section Strategy by class of groups
#!
#! The finitely presented group $Q$ is not a good group to compute in. The
#! package replaces it by a group in which one can compute (a pc group, a pcp
#! group or a subgroup of a direct product), choosing the construction from the
#! class of $G$. In every case the embeddings of the copies of $G$ are kept.
#!
#! For $\chi(G)$ (<Ref Attr="SidkiChiGroup" Label="for IsGroup"/>) the methods are tried in this
#! order:
#! <List>
#! <Item>$G$ finite or polycyclic, generated by two elements, with trivial Schur
#!   multiplier: then $R(G) = 1$ (Kochloukova and Mendon&#xE7;a, 2021,
#!   Lemma 9.3) and $W(G)/R(G) \cong M(G) = 1$ (Rocco, 1982), so $\chi(G)$ is
#!   the subgroup $T(G)$ of $G \times G \times G$ of Sidki (1980). This
#!   includes non-solvable groups, such as $PSL(2,8)$.</Item>
#! <Item>$G$ finite and perfect: $\chi(G) = T(\tilde G)$, where $\tilde G$ is
#!   the universal covering group of $G$ (Sidki, 1980, Theorem 4.4.1).</Item>
#! <Item>$G$ an infinite polycyclic group generated by two elements: $\chi(G)$
#!   is the group $\tau(G) = \tau^0(G)$ of Dias and Rocco, which is
#!   $\chi(G)/R(G)$, and $R(G) = 1$. For other polycyclic groups,
#!   <Ref Attr="SidkiChiModRGroup" Label="for IsGroup"/> gives $\chi(G)/R(G)$.</Item>
#! <Item>$G$ a finite $p$-group: $\chi(G)$ is a finite $p$-group (Sidki), so it
#!   is the largest $p$-quotient of $Q$, computed by <C>EpimorphismPGroup</C>
#!   without a bound on the class.</Item>
#! <Item>$G$ finite and solvable: $\chi(G)$ is a finite solvable group whose
#!   order involves only the primes dividing $|G|$ (Sidki), so it is the
#!   largest such solvable quotient of $Q$, computed by
#!   <C>EpimorphismSolvableQuotient</C>.</Item>
#! </List>
#! For other groups, <Ref Attr="SidkiChiModWGroup" Label="for IsGroup"/> gives $\chi(G)/W(G)$ as a
#! subgroup of $G \times G \times G$.
#!
#! For $\nu(G)$ (<Ref Attr="RoccoNuGroup" Label="for IsGroup"/>): if $G$ is a finite $p$-group,
#! Rocco's presentation (1994), with $x$, $y$ in a pc generating sequence, and
#! the largest $p$-quotient; if $G$ is finite solvable or polycyclic, the
#! nonabelian tensor square of the package Polycyclic (Eick and Nickel).
#! For $\tau^q(G)$ (<Ref Oper="RoccoTauGroup" Label="for IsGroup, IsInt"/>): a polycyclic presentation
#! following Dias and Rocco, for $G$ finite solvable or polycyclic. For
#! $\chi(n,H)$ (<Ref Oper="SidkiChiNGroup" Label="for IsGroup, IsPosInt"/>): $T_n(\tilde H)$ if $H$ is finite
#! and perfect, and the largest $p$-quotient if $H$ is a finite $p$-group.
#!
#! One example of each case:
#! @BeginExampleSession
#! gap> SetInfoLevel( InfoRoccoSidki, 0 );
#! gap> Size( SidkiChiGroup( SymmetricGroup( 3 ) ) );    # T(G) in G x G x G
#! 108
#! gap> Size( SidkiChiGroup( AlternatingGroup( 5 ) ) );  # perfect: T(H~)
#! 432000
#! gap> C := SidkiChiGroup( DihedralPcpGroup( 0 ) );;    # infinite: tau(G)
#! gap> IsFinite( C );  HirschLength( C );
#! false
#! 3
#! gap> Size( SidkiChiGroup( DihedralGroup( 8 ) ) );     # p-group
#! 256
#! gap> Size( SidkiChiGroup( AlternatingGroup( 4 ) ) );  # finite solvable
#! 1152
#! gap> Size( RoccoNuGroup( DihedralGroup( 8 ) ) );      # Rocco 1994
#! 2048
#! gap> Size( RoccoNuGroup( SymmetricGroup( 3 ) ) );     # Polycyclic
#! 216
#! @EndExampleSession

# Internal: the quotient of the free product of copies of G by relations,
# with the embeddings carried through. Documented in the code only.
DeclareGlobalFunction( "RS_FreeProductQuotient" );
DeclareGlobalFunction( "RS_PQuotientWithEmbeddings" );
DeclareGlobalFunction( "RS_SolvableQuotientWithEmbeddings" );
DeclareGlobalFunction( "RS_QuotientWithEmbeddings" );
